Draw menu and tooltip shadows through KWindowShadow

QtCurve gives the shadow tiles to the window manager in
qt6/style/shadowhelper.cpp. The old code put the tiles in the
_KDE_NET_WM_SHADOW property of the X window. A native Wayland client makes
no XCB connection, so qtcX11Enabled() is false and the style installs
nothing. Menus, tooltips, combo box lists and detached tool bars therefore
get no shadow on a Wayland session.

KWindowShadow from KF6WindowSystem carries the same eight tiles and the same
four paddings to the compositor on X11 and on Wayland. The qt6 style already
links KF6::WindowSystem, so the port adds no dependency. QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDE
selects the route: a build without KDE support keeps the X property code in
installShadows(), the new name of installX11Shadows().

The art does not change. qtcShadowCreate() still draws the tiles with the
arguments of qtcX11ShadowInit(): the greys 0.4 and 0.2, radius 4. The
gradient length comes from the shadowSize key, 30 pixels by default, and the
padding is one pixel less. The new code installs no shadow for size zero.

qtcShadowCreate() writes premultiplied pixels in the QTC_PIXEL_QT byte
order. That is the layout of QImage::Format_ARGB32_Premultiplied on a little
endian machine, so each tile needs no conversion. lib/utils keeps the
function private, so qt6/style/CMakeLists.txt compiles lib/utils/shadow.cpp,
and libqtcurve-utils.so.2 exports nothing new.

The style dialog in qt6/config/qtcurveconfig.cpp keeps the drop shadow size
active off X11, because the KWindowShadow route reads the key on every
platform. The dialog builds with KDE Frameworks 6 alone, so the X property
route never serves it. The tooltip of the box no longer claims X11 alone.

The change sits on the branch work/wayland-shadows of the local tree. The fork
wyn/qtcurve holds that branch. Merge request !5 on system/qtcurve holds that
branch as the source.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

